{
  "id": "nonassociative-learning",
  "term": "nonassociative learning",
  "definition": "Nonassociative learning is a basic form of learning seen across animals in which repeated or significant stimulation changes the strength of responding without involving links between different stimuli or events. In this source, its classic forms are habituation and sensitization, which alter stimulus-response pathways and help filter incoming sensory information and attention.",
